To achieve that professional split where the first part is big and the second part (after the dash) is 20px and 500 weight, follow these two steps.
1. In LoginStyles.js
Add this one new styled component at the bottom of your file. This specifically handles the text after the dash.
export const SubHeadingText = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontSize: 20,           // Your requested size
  fontWeight: 500,        // Your requested weight
  color: theme.palette.text.secondary,
  lineHeight: 1.4,
  marginTop: theme.spacing(0.5),
  marginBottom: theme.spacing(2),
  display: "block",       // Ensures it sits on its own line
}));

2. In LoginPort.js
We need to do two things: Import the new style and Split the text.
A. Update your Import:
Add SubHeadingText to the list of imports from ./LoginStyles.
import {
  // ... existing imports
  BigHeading,
  SubHeadingText, // Add this here
  SubText,
  // ...
} from "./LoginStyles";

B. Update the JSX (LeftCol section):
Replace your current heading logic with this. It splits the text at the " - " automatically so you don't have to hardcode the words.
{/* LEFT COLUMN */}
<LeftCol>
  {/* Logic to split the title: "Welcome... - enabling..." */}
  <BigHeading>
    {(content?.hero?.welcomeTitle || "").split(" - ")[0]}
  </BigHeading>

  {/* Only shows the second part if the dash exists */}
  {content?.hero?.welcomeTitle?.includes(" - ") && (
    <SubHeadingText>
      {content?.hero?.welcomeTitle.split(" - ")[1]}
    </SubHeadingText>
  )}

  <SubText>{content?.hero?.subText}</SubText>

  {/* Keep the rest of your legal section below as is */}
  <BigHeading sx={{ fontSize: 20, mt: 3 }}>
    {content?.legal?.title}
  </BigHeading>
  <FeatureList>
    {content?.legal?.items.map((item, idx) => (
      <FeatureItem key={idx}>
        <Bullet /> {item}
      </FeatureItem>
    ))}
  </FeatureList>
</LeftCol>

Why this works:
 * The Split: split(" - ")[0] takes everything before the dash for the big heading. split(" - ")[1] takes everything after for the professional 20px text.
 * Safety: The || "" and the .includes check prevent the "Blank Screen" if the JSON content hasn't loaded yet.
 * Cleanliness: By adding display: "block" and a small marginTop in the styles, the two pieces of text will look perfectly aligned and intentional.
